      "body": "**Core question:** How do societies classify, use, distribute and conserve resources?\n\nExpected foundations:\n- Utility, value, technology and time in making something a resource.\n- Natural, human-made and human resources; renewable/non-renewable; actual/potential; ubiquitous/localised; stock/reserve distinctions where relevant.\n- Land, soil, water, natural vegetation and wildlife; degradation and conservation.\n- Minerals, conventional and non-conventional energy; extraction and impacts.\n- Agriculture: subsistence/commercial systems, crop requirements, seasons and regional variation.\n- Industries: inputs, processes, outputs, location factors, industrial systems and environmental impacts.\n- Human resources: population as capability, distribution, composition, education, health and skills.\n\nTeacher emphasis: resources are not merely “things in nature.” Their usefulness depends on knowledge, technology, institutions, access, value and ecological limits. Separate resource existence from economically viable and socially acceptable use.",
