      "kind": "curriculum_or_design_reference",
      "body": "**Core question:** How are people, livelihoods, settlements, resources and networks organised across space, and with what consequences?\n\nMajor areas:\n- Nature, scope and approaches of human geography.\n- Population distribution, density, growth, demographic transition, composition and migration.\n- Human development and multidimensional wellbeing.\n- Primary activities: hunting/gathering, pastoralism, agriculture, mining and related land-use systems.\n- Secondary activities: manufacturing, industrial location and changing production systems.\n- Tertiary, quaternary and quinary activities; services, knowledge and information economies.\n- Transport, communication, trade and global interdependence.\n- Settlements: rural/urban, morphology, functions, hierarchy and urbanisation.\n- India’s population, migration and settlements; land resources, agriculture, water, minerals, energy and industries.\n- Planning and sustainable development; transport and communication; international trade; environmental problems; regional development and disparities.\n- Practical geography: data sources, processing, statistical representation, thematic mapping and spatial information technology/GIS.\n\nTeacher emphasis: avoid reducing human geography to lists of economic sectors. Explain spatial division of labour, accessibility, institutions, technology, historical path dependence, inequality and environmental externalities.",
